    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i'm particularly worried about the \"friction\" in the system though. even with a roundabout, we see these weird, unpredictable spikes in congestion and little pockets of stagnation that the models don't quite catch. what are the main technical causes for that kind of unexpected friction or sudden interruptions in the flow?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Unexpected traffic friction in roundabouts is often caused by driver behavior rather than pure geometric limitations. Key causes include erratic merging or weaving movements, especially during peak times, which disrupt the smooth flow of circulating traffic. Furthermore, improper lane discipline or aggressive gap acceptance by drivers can initiate localized shockwaves, causing these unpredictable pockets of stagnation. These behavioral factors, which static models struggle to capture accurately, are the primary drivers of sudden congestion.",
      "source": "target",
