    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "right, so it's basically human error or poor driving habits. but is there anything else that might be contributing to that sense of \"unseen\" chaos? like, even when the drivers are being relatively standard, there's sometimes a feeling that the system just isn't working as intended, almost like there's some underlying disruption preventing the efficiency we're seeing on paper. any technical terms for those types of systemic irregularities?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "These deeper systemic irregularities often point to issues within traffic stream theory, such as *jam onset* or *capacity breakdown*. Capacity breakdown occurs when the demand exceeds the operational capacity of the entry points, leading to sudden, non-linear drops in throughput, regardless of driver behavior. Another factor is the effect of *upstream queue spillback*, where congestion from an intersecting road backs up into the roundabout approach, effectively shrinking the usable gap acceptance space. These are recognized operational limits that models can only predict under ideal conditions.",
      "source": "target",
